# ============================================================================
# ATLAS AI — ENVIRONMENT TEMPLATE
# ============================================================================
#
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
#   1. Copy it:  cp .env.local.template .env.local
#   2. Fill in the values below (leave optional ones blank if unused).
#   3. Restart the dev server. Nothing here is read until you restart.
#
# NEVER COMMIT .env.local — it is gitignored. This template is safe to commit
# because it contains no real values.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix means the value is sent to the browser and is PUBLIC.
# Anything without that prefix stays on the server. Do not move a secret into
# a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable to "make it work" — that publishes it.
# ============================================================================


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED — Atlas will not run without these four
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Supabase → Settings → API → "Project URL"
# Looks like: https://abcdefghijk.supabase.co
# MISSING: nothing works. No login, no data, every page errors.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# Supabase → Settings → API → "Project API keys" → anon / public
# Long string starting with eyJ...
# MISSING: nobody can sign up or log in. Auth is dead.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Supabase → Settings → API → "Project API keys" → service_role  ← NOT the anon key
# Also starts with eyJ... but is a DIFFERENT, longer key. Click "Reveal" to see it.
# This one bypasses all database security, so it is server-only. Never share it,
# never put it in a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable, never paste it into client code.
# MISSING: login works but nothing saves — no tasks, memories, contacts or invoices.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# console.groq.com → API Keys → Create API Key
# Starts with gsk_
# MISSING: Atlas cannot think. Chat, briefings, CV writing and parsing all fail.
GROQ_API_KEY=

# http://localhost:3000 while developing.
# Your real domain in production, e.g. https://atlas-ai.vercel.app
# No trailing slash.
# MISSING/WRONG: Google sign-in redirects break and Stripe returns to the wrong page.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GOOGLE — Gmail + Calendar agents (optional, one setup covers both)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Full walkthrough: DEPLOYMENT.md section 3
#
# Quick version:
#   console.cloud.google.com → new project
#   → APIs & Services → Library → enable "Gmail API" and "Google Calendar API"
#   → OAuth consent screen → External → add yourself as a Test user
#   → Credentials → Create Credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application
#   → Authorised redirect URIs, add BOTH:
#        http://localhost:3000/api/auth/google/callback
#        https://your-domain.com/api/auth/google/callback

# From the OAuth client you just created.
# MISSING: Email and Calendar agents show as "not connected". Atlas will say
#          plainly that it cannot send mail or book events — it will not pretend.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT FALLBACK ONLY — leave blank in production.
# A single refresh token that makes every user share ONE Google account. Fine on
# your own machine, wrong for real customers, who each connect their own account
# via the Connect button on /settings.
# MISSING: nothing breaks. Users connect their own accounts instead.
GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STRIPE — paid plans (optional)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# dashboard.stripe.com → Developers → API keys → Secret key
# Use the TEST key (sk_test_...) until you are ready to take real money.
# MISSING: the upgrade buttons return a clear "payments not configured" message.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# Same page → Publishable key (pk_test_... or pk_live_...)
# MISSING: nothing breaks today — the current checkout flow does not use it.
#          Included because most Stripe integrations need it eventually.
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Local:      run `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook`
#             and copy the whsec_... it prints.
# Production: Stripe → Developers → Webhooks → your endpoint → Signing secret.
#             Subscribe to: checkout.session.completed,
#             customer.subscription.updated, customer.subscription.deleted
#
# MISSING: THIS IS THE DANGEROUS ONE. Customers can pay successfully and never
#          get upgraded, because nothing records the payment. Do not launch paid
#          plans without it.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MORNING BRIEFING — scheduled daily briefings (optional)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Any long random string — it is a password, not something you look up.
# Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# Add the SAME value to Vercel's environment variables so the scheduled job can
# authenticate itself.
#
# MISSING: the cron endpoint refuses to run at all, rather than leaving an
#          unauthenticated endpoint open that anyone could hammer to burn your
#          Groq credit. Briefings still generate on demand when you open the
#          dashboard — only the automatic 7am run stops.
BRIEFING_CRON_SECRET=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WEATHER — optional, used in the morning briefing
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# openweathermap.org → sign up free → API keys
# The free tier is plenty. New keys take ~10 minutes to activate.
# MISSING: the briefing simply leaves weather out. It deliberately does NOT ask
#          the AI to guess the forecast, because a model has no way to know
#          today's weather and inventing it would be a lie.
OPENWEATHER_API_KEY=
